    "result_text": "The change is implemented and the full suite is green (46 tests, 0 failures, 0 errors; `BUILD SUCCESS`).\n\n## What I did\n\nThe rule belongs to `POST /api/owners`, whose pipeline starts with the body-validation step `ValidateOwnerFields`. I added the future-date check there, alongside the existing required-field/telephone/email validation, so a bad `registrationDate` is reported the same way as any other invalid field.\n\n**`ValidateOwnerFields.java`** \u2014 after the required-field checks:\n\n```java\n// A supplied registrationDate may not be in the future: reject a date later than the\n// server's current date. Absent is accepted (it defaults to today when the owner is built).\nLocalDate registrationDate = request.getRegistrationDate();\nif (registrationDate != null && registrationDate.isAfter(LocalDate.now())) {\n    errors.add(\"registrationDate\");\n}\n```\n\nBecause it feeds the existing `errors` list, a future date throws `OwnerFieldsInvalidException`, which the existing escalation handler renders as **400** with the offending field named in the `errors` array \u2014 no new exception, handler, or YAML wiring needed. An absent `registrationDate` is still accepted and defaults to today in `BuildOwner`, so `Cp07` (past/weekend dates) is unaffected.\n\n## Tests\n\n`Cp33Tests` already encodes exactly this checkpoint (\"no-future-date: reject a supplied registrationDate later than the server date \u2192 400\", posting `2999-01-01`). It now passes, along with the rest of the suite.\n\nOne note: this compares the **supplied** date against `LocalDate.now()` before the business-day roll-forward, which is the natural reading of \"later than the server date.\" A same-day date is accepted.",
